+# Sysop E2E Test Guide — `ccsession tier policy` (`5.0.0`)
+
+- **Capability:** the per-user tier policy of ccsession in Coder workspaces. `CCSESSION_TIER_RULES` (or the older `CCSESSION_ACCESS`) in the workspace env file sets, per Coder username, Coder role or everyone else, the tier a person starts on, the tiers they are offered and whether `/model` lists each model; letter tiers (`tier1a`, `T1a_WINDOW`, `claude-gw-tier1a`) are part of the layout.
+- **Audience:** system operator / administrator
+- **Gate:** the policy is read from the workspace env file `/etc/aidev02/aienv.d/defaults.sh` (`src/superclaude/skills/ccsession-tag/models.py:104,320-328`), a file pushed from the Coder template and changed only through review (`src/superclaude/skills/ccsession-tag/README.md:276,352-354`). ccsession applies it to the workspace owner named by `CODER_WORKSPACE_OWNER_NAME` and `CODER_WORKSPACE_OWNER_ROLES` (`models.py:499-519`), set by the Coder agent and template. `user_access` picks the owner's entry and stops the launch on a broken one (`models.py:522-620`). The limit is advisory: only a gateway key limited to those models enforces it (`README.md:348-352`).
+- **Source of truth:** `src/superclaude/skills/ccsession-tag/models.py:53-104,320-328,376-479,499-637,814-828,843-974,993-1028`; `src/superclaude/skills/ccsession-tag/ccsession:57-70,683-686,801-813,936-971,1015,1402-1435,1483-1489`; `src/superclaude/skills/ccsession-tag/local-gateway-alias-proxy.py:72,123-133,172-203,705-714`; `src/superclaude/skills/ccsession-tag/README.md:272-379`; `src/superclaude/skills/ccsession-tag/ccsession-models.json:2-3,70-121`; `CHANGELOG.md:151-168,182-186`
+- **Est. time:** `Tier A: 3 minutes. Tier B: 15-20 minutes. Tier C: 15-25 minutes.`
+
+## Preconditions
+
+- IronClaude 5.0.0 installed for your user (`superclaude install` done): `superclaude --version` prints `SuperClaude, version 5.0.0`, and `~/.claude/skills/ccsession-tag/` holds the 5.0.0 ccsession (`models.py`, `ccsession`, `ccsession-models.json` version `2026-10-28.1`).
+- A Coder workspace whose env file `/etc/aidev02/aienv.d/defaults.sh` defines tier windows (`T<N>_WINDOW`), so ccsession runs in tier mode (`models.py:469-479`). Tier A and Tier C need it; Tier B does not.
+- Linux with `bash`, `python3`, `curl`, `grep` and `sed` in `/usr/bin` or `/bin`. Tier B runs every command with `PATH=/tmp/e01/bin:/usr/bin:/bin` and nothing else from your environment.
+- `/tmp/e01` does not exist (`[ ! -e /tmp/e01 ] && echo OK` prints `OK`).
+- **Privilege/gate satisfied.** Changing the real policy needs write access to `/etc/aidev02/aienv.d/defaults.sh`, which a workspace user does not have, and a reviewed change to the Coder template that pushes it. Coder role assignment (the roles that reach `CODER_WORKSPACE_OWNER_ROLES`) is a Coder admin action outside this repo. This guide needs neither: it tests the policy from repo code by pointing `AIDEV_AI_DEFAULTS_PATH` at a test env file (`models.py:320-328`) and setting `CODER_WORKSPACE_OWNER_NAME` / `CODER_WORKSPACE_OWNER_ROLES` per command in a throwaway shell environment.
+- **Safety.**
+  - Never edit the real `/etc/aidev02/aienv.d/defaults.sh` for this test. A broken rules line there stops ccsession for every Coder user until it is fixed (`README.md:332-336`). Every rule in this guide lives in a file under `/tmp/e01`.
+  - Tier A only reads. Tier B writes only under `/tmp/e01`, with a throwaway `HOME` and cache (`/tmp/e01/home`, `/tmp/e01/cache`), `CLAUDE_BIN=/bin/false` and `CCSESSION_MODELS_REFRESH=0`: it never starts Claude Code, never starts a gateway shim and never fetches the model data. Every Tier B launch is one the policy refuses, which ends before the shim and the session folder (`ccsession:1483-1489`; the launch stops at `ccsession:948,954,956`).
+  - Tier C copies your real env file into `/tmp/e01/live.sh` (it may hold gateway settings; `/tmp/e01` is made readable only by you, and Cleanup removes it), and runs one Claude Code session with a second throwaway `HOME` (`/tmp/e01/c-home`). That `HOME` has no `settings.json`, so no SuperClaude hooks run: the hooks that write `~/.claude/state/` and `~/.claude/logs/` (`src/superclaude/hooks/hooks.json:18,49,104`) never touch your real `~/.claude`.
+  - Never run `pkill -f local-gateway-alias-proxy.py`: one shim serves every ccsession session of yours with the same settings, so that command cuts all of them (`ccsession:766-768`). Cleanup stops only this guide's shim, by its `/tmp/e01/` path.
+  - `ccsession` starts Claude Code with `--dangerously-skip-permissions --allowedTools=Grep` unless `CC_CLAUDE_FLAGS` is set (`ccsession:78-83,93`). In Tier C, type only the prompts this guide gives.
+
+## What you're verifying
+
+- The rules grammar `<who>=<start tier>[:<tier>,...][+models]`, where `<who>` is a Coder username, `role:<coder role>` or `*` (`models.py:72-79`).
+- Precedence: the owner's username entry, then the first `role:` entry (in file order) matching one of their roles, then `*`; with no match, `CCSESSION_DEFAULT_TIER` and every tier (`models.py:522-541,582-584`).
+- `CCSESSION_ACCESS` is read only when the file has no `CCSESSION_TIER_RULES` line (`models.py:81-85,545-546`).
+- A broken or repeated entry that applies to the owner stops the launch with the reason instead of opening every tier; a malformed piece of the value, or a second line naming the setting, stops every launch (`models.py:384-398,416-433,557-581`).
+- A limited user's `--help`, `--models-status`, `--tier`, `--model` and the shim's `/model` list and routing cover only the allowed tiers (`models.py:630-637,843-935`; `local-gateway-alias-proxy.py:172-203,705-714`).
+- Letter tiers: `tier1a` / `1A` / `T1a_WINDOW` / `claude-gw-tier1a` (`models.py:63-65,489-496,855`; `CHANGELOG.md:156`).
+- The limit is advisory: the owner can undo it from their own environment. That is expected behaviour, not a FAIL (`README.md:348-354`; `CHANGELOG.md:186`).
+
