+### Tier B — the policy from repo code, with throwaway files under /tmp/e01
+
+#### Step 3 — Build the throwaway env, the helpers and a test env file `[LOCAL-WRITE]`
+
+**Do:**
+
+```bash
+[ ! -e /tmp/e01 ] || { echo "/tmp/e01 exists: run Cleanup first"; false; }
+mkdir -p /tmp/e01/home /tmp/e01/cache /tmp/e01/bin && chmod 700 /tmp/e01
+ln -s "$HOME/.claude/skills/ccsession-tag/ccsession" /tmp/e01/bin/ccsession
+printf 'E1_MODELS=%q\n' "$HOME/.claude/skills/ccsession-tag/models.py" > /tmp/e01/e1.sh
+cat >> /tmp/e01/e1.sh <<'EOF'
+e1() {  # clean environment: throwaway HOME and cache, the test env file, no Claude, no network
+  env -i PATH=/tmp/e01/bin:/usr/bin:/bin HOME=/tmp/e01/home XDG_CACHE_HOME=/tmp/e01/cache \
+    AIDEV_AI_DEFAULTS_PATH=/tmp/e01/defaults.sh CCSESSION_MODELS_REFRESH=0 \
+    CLAUDE_BIN=/bin/false PYTHONDONTWRITEBYTECODE=1 "$@"
+}
+rule() {  # rule <name> <line>...: /tmp/e01/<name>.sh = the tier settings plus the given lines
+  local f="/tmp/e01/$1.sh"; shift
+  cp /tmp/e01/tiers.sh "$f" && printf '%s\n' "$@" >> "$f"
+}
+EOF
+source /tmp/e01/e1.sh
+{
+  echo 'export CCSESSION_DEFAULT_TIER=tier2'
+  for t in 0:2 1a:2 1b:3 2:6 3:6 4:1; do
+    n=${t%:*}; echo "export T${n}_WINDOW=400000"
+    for i in $(seq 1 "${t#*:}"); do
+      printf 'export T%sModel%02d=e01-t%s-m%d\nexport T%sModel%02d_WINDOW=400000\n' "$n" "$i" "$n" "$i" "$n" "$i"
+    done
+  done
+} > /tmp/e01/tiers.sh
+rule defaults 'export CCSESSION_TIER_RULES="alice=tier1b:tier1b,tier2 role:auditor=tier1a+models *=tier2:tier2,tier3,tier4"'
+e1 python3 "$E1_MODELS" tier-mode; echo "exit=$?"
+e1 ccsession --models-status
+```
+
+**Expected result:**
+
+- `exit=0`: the test file defines tier windows, so tier mode is on (`models.py:469-479,841-842`). The slot names follow the 5.0.0 layout: tier0 2 models, tier1a 2, tier1b 3, tier2 6, tier3 6, tier4 1 (`ccsession-models.json:70-121`); a missing slot would stop the launch with `T<N>Model0M is not set (needed by tier<N>)` (`models.py:661-664`).
+- The status block (`models.py:993-1025`):
